      "file": "items/ex-pc-chain-versus-collider-separating-sets.md",
      "item_id": "ex-pc-chain-versus-collider-separating-sets",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** Independent $N(0,1)$ exogenous variables and the two recursive Gaussian models in the Example.\n\n[F1] Finite independent sums and affine shifts have the stated characteristic-function formulas. [[def-independent-random-elements]] [[lem-characteristic-functions-under-affine-maps-and-independent-sums]]\n\n[F2] The standard normal has density $\\phi$, scalar normal laws have characteristic function $\\exp(i\\mu t-\\sigma^2t^2/2)$, and equal scalar characteristic functions determine equal laws. [[def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws]] [[lem-characteristic-function-of-a-normal-law]] [[thm-uniqueness-of-a-law-from-its-characteristic-function]]\n\n[F3] A finite vector is multivariate normal when every scalar linear projection has its corresponding normal law. [[def-multivariate-normal-law]]\n\n[F4] For a nondegenerate Gaussian vector, zero conditional covariance between two coordinates is equivalent to conditional independence given the conditioning coordinates. [[thm-gaussian-zero-partial-correlation-is-conditional-independence]]\n\n[F5] PC deletes a pair at the first conditioning level with a separating set, records that set, orients an unshielded triple as a collider exactly when the middle vertex is absent from its recorded set, then applies R1–R3. [[alg-pc-causal-discovery]]\n\n[F6] The middle vertex of an unshielded triple lies outside a valid separating set exactly when the triple is a collider. [[lem-pc-separating-set-collider]]\n\n[F7] A Gaussian law is faithful to a DAG when its conditional independences agree exactly with that DAG's d-separations. [[def-causal-discovery-oracle-regime]]\n\n[F8] A recursive SCM is Markovian when its exogenous variables are mutually independent; its displayed structural functions define the endogenous variables along the graph. [[def-recursive-structural-causal-model-and-dag]]\n\n[F9] Under the causally sufficient perfect-oracle regime, population PC terminates and returns the DAG's CPDAG. [[thm-pc-oracle-correctness]]\n\n[F10] D-separation requires every path between the queried vertex sets to be blocked by the conditioning set. [[def-d-separation-in-a-dag]]\n\n[A1] AC is assumed by the supplied Gaussian probability interfaces. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]\n\n\n\n**Proof technique:** direct covariance calculation and the population PC search.\n\n1.1 Let $W_1,\\ldots,W_m$ be a finite independent family of scalar normal variables with means $\\mu_r$ and variances $\\sigma_r^2$. For any real coefficients $a_1,\\ldots,a_m,b$, [F1]–[F2] give the characteristic function of $b+\\sum_r a_rW_r$ as $\\exp\\!\\left(it(b+\\sum_r a_r\\mu_r)-\\frac{t^2}{2}\\sum_r a_r^2\\sigma_r^2\\right)$; scalar uniqueness identifies this as the normal law with that mean and variance. Every scalar projection of a finite affine image of $W=(W_1,\\ldots,W_m)$ is such a sum, so [F3] proves that every such image is multivariate normal, including when a projection has variance zero. [F1, F2, F3, A1, construct]\n\n2.1 The chain model's density is $\\phi(x)\\phi(y-x)\\phi(z-y)$, where $\\phi$ is the standard-normal density. Its factors are exactly the parent probability kernels $K_X(dx)=N(0,1)(dx)$, $K_Y(x,dy)=N(x,1)(dy)$, and $K_Z(y,dz)=N(y,1)(dz)$, so the full-joint law factorizes in the topological order $X,Y,Z$. All three vertices are observed and [F8] gives the Markovian SCM condition. Its moments are $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=1$, $\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=2$, $\\operatorname{Var}(Z)=3$, and distinct covariances $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=1$, $\\operatorname{Cov}(Y,Z)=2$, $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Z)=1$. The vector is multivariate normal by step 1.1, and its covariance matrix is positive definite because $(X,Y,Z)$ is an invertible triangular linear transform of $(U_X,U_Y,U_Z)$. The conditional covariance formula gives $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Z\\mid Y)=1-\\frac{1\\cdot2}{2}=0,\\quad \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y\\mid Z)=1-\\frac{1\\cdot2}{3}=\\frac13,\\quad \\operatorname{Cov}(Y,Z\\mid X)=2-\\frac{1\\cdot1}{1}=1.$ Together with the nonzero marginal covariances, [F4] shows that the only conditional independence between distinct variables is $X\\perp Z\\mid Y$. By [F10], the only d-separated vertex pair is also $X,Z$ given $Y$: the adjacent pairs have an active one-edge path, and the $X$–$Z$ path is blocked only by conditioning on $Y$. Any conditional independence between vertex sets would imply the corresponding coordinate independences, so the set-level independence and d-separation models also agree. This verifies faithfulness in [F7]. [given, F1, F2, F4, F7, F8, F10, A1, algebra]\n\n2.2 The collider model's density is $\\phi(x)\\phi(z)\\phi(y-x-z)$. Its factors are exactly the parent probability kernels $K_X(dx)=N(0,1)(dx)$, $K_Z(dz)=N(0,1)(dz)$, and $K_Y(x,z,dy)=N(x+z,1)(dy)$, so the full-joint law factorizes in the topological order $X,Z,Y$. All vertices are observed and [F8] gives the Markovian SCM condition. Its variances are $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\operatorname{Var}(Z)=1$, $\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=3$, its covariances are $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\\operatorname{Cov}(Z,Y)=1$, and $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Z)=0$. The vector is multivariate normal by step 1.1, and this covariance matrix is positive definite by the invertible triangular map from the exogenous variables. The conditional covariances are $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Z\\mid Y)=0-\\frac{1\\cdot1}{3}=-\\frac13,\\quad \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y\\mid Z)=1,\\quad \\operatorname{Cov}(Y,Z\\mid X)=1.$ Therefore [F4] shows that $X\\perp Z$ marginally, but there is no conditional independence after conditioning on the third variable and no independence for an adjacent pair. By [F10], this is exactly the collider DAG's d-separation list: the collider path is blocked unconditioned and opened by conditioning on $Y$, while each adjacent pair has an active one-edge path. Any set conditional independence would imply coordinate independences, so [F7] holds at the set level as well. [given, F1, F2, F4, F7, F8, F10, A1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Thus PC does not delete any pair at level $0$. At level $1$, $X-Z$ is deleted and its separator is recorded as $\\{Y\\}$; $X-Y$ and $Y-Z$ remain because step 2.1 shows that each is dependent both marginally and conditional on the third variable. The sole unshielded triple has its middle vertex in the separator, so [F5] or [F6] leaves both edges undirected. No Meek rule applies without a directed edge, and the output is the undirected chain CPDAG. The run terminates under [F9], since step 2.1 verifies the chain model's discovery-regime hypotheses. [F5, F6, F9, step 2.1, cases]\n\n4.1 PC deletes $X-Z$ at level $0$ and records the empty separator. It retains $X-Y$ and $Y-Z$ by their nonzero marginal covariances. Since $Y$ is absent from the separator, [F5] orients $X\\to Y\\leftarrow Z$; the output is the collider CPDAG. The run terminates under [F9], since step 2.2 verifies the collider model's discovery-regime hypotheses. These calculations verify both claimed outputs without relying on the diagrams. [F4, F5, F9, step 2.2, cases] ∎"
    },
    {
      "file": "items/ex-pc-latent-confounding-limitations.md",
      "item_id": "ex-pc-latent-confounding-limitations",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** Independent Gaussian exogenous variables in the latent model and independent variables $V_X,\\varepsilon$ in the direct-edge model.\n\n[F1] A recursive structural causal model may distinguish observed and latent vertices; its Markovian case uses mutually independent exogenous variables. [[def-recursive-structural-causal-model-and-dag]]\n\n[F2] The causally sufficient discovery regime requires every graph vertex to be observed and the full-joint law to have a parent-kernel factorization, so an omitted latent vertex is outside that regime. [[def-causal-discovery-oracle-regime]]\n\n[F3] Independent scalar normal inputs have product characteristic functions, and finite affine sums use the scalar normal characteristic formula and scalar uniqueness. [[def-independent-random-elements]] [[def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws]] [[lem-characteristic-functions-under-affine-maps-and-independent-sums]] [[lem-characteristic-function-of-a-normal-law]] [[thm-uniqueness-of-a-law-from-its-characteristic-function]]\n\n[F4] A finite vector is multivariate normal when every scalar linear projection has its corresponding normal law. [[def-multivariate-normal-law]]\n\n[F5] A multivariate normal law has characteristic function $\\exp(it\\cdot m-t^T\\Sigma t/2)$, and this function uniquely determines its vector law. [[lem-characteristic-function-of-a-multivariate-normal-law]]\n\n[F6] DAGs with the same skeleton and no unshielded colliders are Markov equivalent. [[thm-dag-markov-equivalence-skeleton-vstructures]]\n\n[F7] With two vertices, a CPDAG leaves the only edge undirected when both orientations occur among equivalent DAGs. [[def-cpdag-markov-equivalence-class]]\n\n[F8] PC retains an edge unless a tested conditioning set establishes independence; with two vertices it has no unshielded triples or Meek-rule configurations. [[alg-pc-causal-discovery]]\n\n[F9] Under the causally sufficient perfect-oracle regime, population PC terminates and returns the DAG's CPDAG. [[thm-pc-oracle-correctness]]\n\n[A1] AC is assumed by the Gaussian-law probability interfaces. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]\n\n\n\n**Proof technique:** covariance comparison and direct inspection of the two-vertex PC search.\n\n1.1 Let $W_1,\\ldots,W_m$ be a finite independent family of scalar normal variables with means $\\mu_r$ and variances $\\sigma_r^2$. For real coefficients $a_1,\\ldots,a_m,b$, [F3] gives the characteristic function of $b+\\sum_r a_rW_r$ as $\\exp\\!\\left(it(b+\\sum_r a_r\\mu_r)-\\frac{t^2}{2}\\sum_r a_r^2\\sigma_r^2\\right)$; scalar uniqueness identifies this as the normal law with that mean and variance. Every scalar projection of a finite affine image of $W=(W_1,\\ldots,W_m)$ is such a sum, so [F4] proves that every such image is multivariate normal, including when a projection has variance zero. [F3, F4, A1, construct]\n\n2.1 In the latent model, $\\mathbb E(X,Y)=(0,0)$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=2$, and $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=1$, by independence of $L,U_X,U_Y$. In the direct-edge model, $\\mathbb E(X,Y)=(0,0)$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=2$, $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Var}(X)=1$, and $\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=\\tfrac14(2)+\\tfrac32=2$. Both vectors are Gaussian by step 1.1 and have the same positive-definite covariance matrix $\\Sigma=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}$, whose leading principal minors are $2$ and $3$. By [F5], both vector characteristic functions equal $\\exp(-t^T\\Sigma t/2)$ for every $t\\in\\mathbb R^2$; the uniqueness clause of [F5] gives equality of the full observed laws. [given, F5, step 1.1, A1, algebra]\n\n3.1 The latent model is a finite recursive Markovian SCM with observed set $\\{X,Y\\}$ and omitted latent vertex $L$, so it does not satisfy [F2]. The direct-edge model has observed set $\\{X,Y\\}$ and independent exogenous variables; its full-joint law factors as $N(0,2)(dx)N(x/2,3/2)(dy)$, the root probability kernel for $X$ followed by the probability kernel for $Y$ given its parent $X$. In its two-vertex graph $X\\to Y$, the only pair of nonempty disjoint endpoint sets is $\\{X\\},\\{Y\\}$, which is not d-separated because of the direct path; step 2.1 gives $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=1$, so they are not independent. Thus this law is faithful to the direct-edge DAG, and the direct-edge model satisfies the observed-only Markovian factorization and faithfulness conditions in the discovery regime. Since the observed laws agree by step 2.1, no procedure using only that law can distinguish the two causal explanations. [F1, F2, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 The common observed law has $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=1\\ne0$, so $X,Y$ are dependent and the perfect oracle does not delete their only edge. With only two vertices there is no unshielded triple and none of R1–R3 applies; by [F9] the regime-compliant run terminates, and its computed output is $X-Y$. Reversing the single edge in the direct-edge DAG keeps the same skeleton and no unshielded collider, so [F6] proves the reverse DAG is equivalent; [F7] therefore gives the same undirected CPDAG. The PC output reports adjacency but cannot recover the latent-versus-direct causal distinction. [F6, F7, F8, F9, step 2.1, step 3.1, A1, algebra] ∎"
    },
    {
